on the outskirts of cambridge scientists  are tracking the spread of the new  variant of the virus  10 000 samples a week taken from people  having coveted tests  are analyzed by the welcome sanger  institute  they've spotted mutations that not only  make the virus more transmissible  but also it's now been revealed more  deadly  if you took somebody in their 60s a man  in their 60s  the average risk is that for a thousand  people  who got infected roughly 10  would be expected to unfortunately die  with the virus with the new variant for  a thousand people  infected roughly 13 or 14 people  might be expected to die genetic  analysis of virus samples has also shown  that the south african variant has now  been detected in at least 44 people in  the uk  research suggests it may evade some  antibodies  and with another version in brazil  significant mutations that give the  virus an  edge seem to be cropping up faster  scientists say that's because there's  lots of cases  in general we think that it's just  simply that there's just so much virus  around  and so many different people that the  the probability of one of these  mutations occurring  is increased by the fact we've got lots  of virus so really the  the real answer for this is getting the  number of cases down  since the virus started spreading in  wuhan it's been mutating every two weeks  into distinct but related versions  visualized as an increasingly crowded  family tree  by the next strain tracker most  mutations don't matter  only recently have new more significant  descendants  evolved in the uk south africa and  brazil  some of the significant mutations  cropping up in the new variants of the  virus here in the uk  in south africa and brazil are the same  scientists call it convergent evolution  just as birds and bats have both evolved  wings independently  to fly so the virus is coming out with  the same strategy  for evading the immune system new  research in cambridge shows how fast the  virus can evolve  inside the bodies of people with poorly  functioning immune systems  a man treated for 100 days became an  incubator  with a virus changing in response to  every treatment  within days you would see shifts big big  population shifts and it tells you that  there's so much virus turnover in  individuals as i've been saying  that this virus is replicating at huge  rates the really big changes in the  virus came when  he was given convalescent plasma and so  contains many different types of  antibodies  every time an infusion was given the  virus population completely changed  all the evidence shows the vaccines are  effective against the uk  variant five and a half million people  have so far had their first dose  and because the vaccines are being  targeted at those most at risk  deaths should soon start to fall despite  the new variant  thomas more sky news
 

